nodecode-defend is a rewrite of PerryLink's PerryLink/dsh-defend:

  https://github.com/PerryLink/dsh-defend
  commit 6c1e28e5fa29d2473fe21d32b883091af681ba21
  Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) PerryLink (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the scanner and its tiers. The original's rule tables
(src/detect/rules.ts, src/detect/secrets.ts) are carried as Common Lisp rule
rows with the same ids, families, categories and severities -- a trimmed
subset, about half the rows, keeping every family and every secret grammar;
the needles and tolerant patterns are cl-ppcre scanners where the original
used an Aho-Corasick automaton plus regexes. The entropy gate over secret
candidates, the "a critical secret is blocked whatever secret_action says"
rule, the allow/ask/block tiers per family, the memory-only report that
records a rule and never the matched text, and the two verbs (a scan, and a
report the /defend command also prints) all come across unchanged.

What was left out, and why: the original hooked three DeepSeek Harness seams
(agent/pre-step for inbound messages, tools/pre-execute for tool arguments,
tools/post-execute for tool results) and could put an approval card up from
the harness's approval service. Nodecode has one tool, eval, so only the
pre-execute seam exists: this cell hooks the :tool point, reads the call's
form and the strings it carries, and asks the operator through the core's own
question board (Allow once / Deny), refusing when nobody answers. The
post-execute and pre-step seams have no counterpart here. The upstream
session-audit event (defend/detection, with its marker probing) became the
in-memory ring buffer /defend and defend_report read, since there is no
session event stream to append to. The NFKC normalization that closed the
lookalike-Unicode bypass is not carried: the scan folds case only, so a
payload written in lookalike characters can slip a needle. The JSON report
the original's defend_report tool answered is kept as JSON here; the
original's Aho-Corasick matcher is replaced by per-needle scanning, which is
slower but reads the same needles.
